   "text": "I read this as a glucosamine-type pyranose with a phosphate and three 3-(decanoyloxy)tetradecanoyl chains, plus three sodium counterions. This is a lipid A mimetic.\n\n**Structure read from the drawing**\n- **Ring:** a six-membered pyranose with ring oxygen, bearing a CH2OH at C5, a phosphate at C4, an acyloxy group at C3, an acylamino group at C2, and an exocyclic ether oxygen at the anomeric C1.\n- **Serine unit:** the C1 oxygen is linked to CH2\u2013CH(NH\u2013acyl)\u2013COO\u207b, i.e. an O-glycosylated serine carboxylate.\n- **Acyl chains:** three identical 3-(decanoyloxy)tetradecanoyl groups. One is on the C3 oxygen, one on the C2 nitrogen, and one on the serine nitrogen.\n  - Each is C(=O)CH2\u2013CH(O\u2013decanoyl)\u2013(CH2)10CH3. The decanoyl group is C(=O) followed by 9 carbons, and the tetradecanoyl tail after C3 is 11 carbons.\n- **Phosphate and counterions:** the phosphate carries two negative charges and the carboxylate one, balanced by three Na\u207a.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nI assigned each centre from the wedges and hashes at the ring carbons, the serine \u03b1-carbon and the three acyl C3 carbons. All three acyl C3 centres work out as R (the natural configuration). The wedge directions at the serine \u03b1-carbon and the acyl C3 centres were hard to make out in the drawing, so those assignments are less certain.\n\nSMILES: OC[C@@H]1O[C@@H](OC[C@H](NC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)C([O-])=O)[C@H](NC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H](OC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H]1OP([O-])([O-])=O.[Na+].[Na+].[Na+]"
